   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**2.1 Context and Aim**\n",
    "\n",
    "This section describes the selection and checking of census data on household composition and weekly rent in Australia in 2021 and New Zealand in 2023. The data will support comparisons of rent patterns across household types and geographic areas for research questions 1 and 2."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**2.2 Dataset Overview**\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Source (AUS)**: Australian Bureau of Statistics (ABS) Census TableBuilder.\n",
    "2. **Source (NZ)**: Stats NZ Aotearoa Data Explorer.\n",
    "3. **Years**: Australia 2021 and New Zealand 2023.\n",
    "4. **Population of interest**: Households in rented occupied private dwellings, using the scope of each country's census table.\n",
    "5. **Unit**: The Australian table contains dwelling counts classified by household type. The NZ table contains household counts. Neither contains individual rent observations. Weekly rent categories are in AUD and NZD respectively.\n",
    "6. **Tables used**:\n",
    "     * AUS: Family household composition (Dwelling), remoteness areas, rent (weekly) ranges, with tenure restricted to Rented.\n",
    "     * NZ: CEN23_HOU_022, Weekly rent paid by household, total household income, household composition and region.\n",
    "\n",
    "The two input files are:\n",
    "* `2021Census_GCP_AUS.csv`\n",
    "* `NZ,CEN23_HOU_022.csv`"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**2.3 Procedure**\n",
    "\n",
    "*Australia (2021 Census data):*\n",
    "1. Open and register at ABS TableBuilder using the university's email address.\n",
    "2. Confirm whether you were granted Census TableBuilder **Pro** access (check 'My profile summary').\n",
    "3. On the **Datasets** page, expand the Census folders using the small triangles. Select **2021 Census - counting dwellings, place of enumeration**, then click **New table**.\n",
    "4. In the table screen, locate to **Dataset search** at the bottom of the left panel. Type **HCFMD** and press **Enter**. This finds **HCFMD Family Household Composition (Dwelling)**.\n",
    "5. Click the arrow beside that variable to open **Select a level**. Select the **2-digit level**, then click **Add to Row**. Include all household categories at this level.\n",
    "6. Clear the search using **X**. Search for **Remoteness Areas (National) (EN)** and press **Enter**. Select all categories for this variable, then click **Add to Row** again. The table should now have household type first and remoteness area second.\n",
    "7. Clear the search again. Search for **RNTRD Rent (weekly) Ranges**. Select all rent categories and click **Add to Column**.\n",
    "8. Search for **TEND Tenure Type**. Expand its categories, tick **Rented** only, and click **Add to Filder**. Check that **Rented** appears in the filters above the table.\n",
    "9. Check that the table is counting **Dwelling Records**. Retain the original categories, including **Not stated** and **Not applicable**, in the download.\n",
    "10. For each row and column variable, open the three-dot menu beside its name and ensure **Total** is enabled. Under **Options**, leave percentages set to **None** so the table contains counts.\n",
    "11. Click **Retrieve Data** to populate the table. Check that household types and remoteness areas appear down the rows, with rent ranges across columns.\n",
    "12. At the top right, open **Download table** and select **(.csv)**. The file will be saved in device download folder. Keep the downloaded csv and its metadata unchanged for data cleaning.\n",
    "\n",
    "*New Zealand (2023 Census data):*\n",
    "\n",
    "13. Open Aotearoa Data Explorer, and search **Weekly rent paid by household, total household income, and household composition for households in rented occupied private dwellings, (RC, TALB, SA2, Health), 2013, 2018, and 2023 Censuses**. This is essentially **CEN23_HOU_022**.\n",
    "14. Under **Refine your data selection**, click **Census year**. Check that **2023** is selected, and **2013** and **2018** are not selected.\n",
    "15. Click **Area**. Check that only the following **18 areas** are selected:\n",
    "     * Total - New Zealand by regional council\n",
    "     * Northland, Auckland, Waikato, Bay of Plenty, Gisborne, Hawke's Bay, Taranaki, Manawatu-Whanganui, Wellington, Tasman, Nelson, Marlborough, West Coast, Canterbury, Otago and Southland regions\n",
    "     * Area Outside Region\n",
    "16. Click **Total household income**. Keep only **Total - total household income** selected.\n",
    "17. Leave **Household composition** and **Weekly rent paid by household** unrestricted so all the categories are included.\n",
    "18. Check the selection summary above the table, it should show 1,584 data points.\n",
    "19. Click **Download** above the table, then choose **Filtered data in tabular text (CSV)**. The file will be saved in device download folder.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Save and organize the downloaded files*\n",
    "\n",
    "20. Open **Google Drive** and select **My Drive**. \n",
    "21. Create a new folder and named **CITS2402_assignment1**.\n",
    "22. Inside **CITS2402_assignment1**, create new folders named **AUS_DATA_2021** and **NZ_DATA_2023**.\n",
    "23. Open **AUS_DATA_2021** and upload the Australian CSV from downloads, the file **2021Census_GCP_AUS.csv**\n",
    "24. Return to **NZ_DATA_2023** and upload the New Zealand CSV from downloads, the file **NZ,CEN23_HOU_022.csv**."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1b180e42",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**2.4 Assumptions for Data Preparation**\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Only renting households are included in the analysis; the study focuses on households living in rented occupied private dwellings. Households that own their homes are excluded because they do not belong to the population being studied. National totals, unclassified household types and special geographic categories are kept separate from the household and area groups being compared. This means the findings describe renting households in selected groups, not all households in Australia or New Zealand.\n",
    "2. The downloaded NZ rent table combines couple-only families, couples with children and one-parent families into the broader category One-family household. Similarly, Australia categories are combined into a similar broad one-family group. However, the countries' definitions are not identcal, so these should be treated as broad comparison groups. Findings should not suggest that every household is classified in exactly the same way in both countries. For example, siblings living togetehr can form an Australian family, whereas New Zealand can classify them as an multi-person household.\n",
    "3. Categories such as **Not stated**, **Not applicable** and **Not elsewhere included** are excluded when calculating the distribution of rent. Values such as **c..** are treated as missing, never as zero.\n",
    "4. Totals and their component categories must not be counted together. A total already includes its component categories, for example, adding a **Total households** count to the individual household type counts would count the same households twice. However, small differences between sum of individual categories and a published total can occur due to ABS protecting confidentiality and Stats NZ applying roundings. For the analysis, percentages use the sum of selected classified rent bands consistently, and the counts are not changed to force agreement with published totals. But any large discrepacy is still and should be investigated.\n",
    "5. Rent bands limit what can be calculated precisely. The exported data give counts within rent ranges, rather than each household's specific weekly rent. An exact median cannot be determined, but later work can identify a median band or clearly label an estimated median. For example, the percentage paying atleast $400 per week can be calculated without estimating individual rents because $400 is a boundary in both datasets. In addition, AUD and NZD amounts are not directly comparable, therefore results should clearly describe clear country, currency and year labels.\n",
    "6. Australian remoteness categories and NZ regions represent different geographic divisions. Australia's remoteness categories describe relative geographic access to services, including Major Cities, Inner Regional, Outer Regional, Remote and Very Remote areas. NZ regional councils cover regions that can contain both urban and rural communities. Differences in the number, size and composition of these areas also affect the observed gaps.\n",
    "7. Weekly rent measures housing cost, not affordability. It does not show how easily the household can afford that payment. Two households paying the same rent may experience very different financial pressure because their incomes differ. Assessing affordability requires rent to be considered along with income.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d3083a57",
   "metadata": {},
